---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Demo from "../../../components/Demo.astro";
import { projectForm, formChoices, formError, formStates } from "../../../examples/forms";
import { url } from "../../../lib/urls";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Forms"
  description="Native fields with visible labels, helpful descriptions, and error messages that tell people what to do next."
  section="Components"
  accent="moss"
  source="website/src/pages/docs/components/forms.astro"
>
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="a-complete-form">Start with a complete field</h2>
    <p>
      <code>lf-field</code> groups a label, a control, and its supporting text. Match the label’s <code
        >for</code
      > with a unique field <code>id</code>. Associate hints and errors using <code
        >aria-describedby</code
      >. A placeholder is not a replacement for a label.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a local demonstration, not a sign-up form. Use sample values. With JavaScript,
      submission validates the fields and shows a result without sending data. Without JavaScript,
      the native GET form reloads this page with sample values in the URL.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Demo id="project-form" title="Text, select, textarea, and checkbox" code={projectForm} />
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <p>
      <code>data-demo-form</code> and <code>data-demo-status</code> belong to this documentation. Remove
      them when copying the markup and provide your own form action or submission handler. The library
      does not submit, store, or validate application data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use input types and autocomplete tokens that fit real fields—such as
      <code>type="email"</code> and <code>autocomplete="email"</code> for a contact address. This generic
      project-name demo turns autocomplete off; that is not a recommendation for personal-information
      fields.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="choices">Checkboxes and radio groups</h2>
      <p>
        Wrap the input and text in <code>lf-choice</code> to make the whole label usable. Give related
        radios the same <code>name</code> and group them with a <code>fieldset</code> and descriptive
        <code>legend</code>. The browser supplies radio-group arrow-key behavior.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Demo id="form-choices" title="A native radio group" code={formChoices} />
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="errors">Make errors understandable</h2>
      <p>
        Pair <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> with specific error text and a description relationship.
        This example starts with an invalid sample value. Type <code>reader@example.com</code> to see
        the error clear.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Demo id="validation-example" title="An error that can be corrected" code={formError} />
    <noscript>
      <p class="docs-inline-note">
        Without JavaScript, this error preview is a static example. Native email validation still
        works when you submit.
      </p>
    </noscript>
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <p>
        The demo handler updates <code>aria-invalid</code>, hides or reveals the error, and keeps <code
          >aria-describedby</code
        > in sync. In your application, choose a considerate validation timing and do not announce an
        assertive error on every keystroke.
      </p>
      <p>
        Native browser validation is useful, but it does not replace server-side validation.
        Preserve entered values after errors and help people find the affected fields. For longer
        forms, consider a linked error summary.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-section">
    <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
      <h2 id="read-only-and-disabled">Read-only is not disabled</h2>
      <p>
        A read-only text field remains focusable and selectable, and a named read-only field is
        submitted with the form. A disabled control is not focusable or submitted. Choose based on
        behavior, not only appearance.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Demo id="form-states" title="Two different native states" code={formStates} />
  </section>

  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="field-checklist">Before shipping</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use unique IDs when an example appears more than once.</li>
      <li>Mark required fields in visible text as well as with <code>required</code>.</li>
      <li>Keep a logical tab order; do not use positive <code>tabindex</code> values.</li>
      <li>Test labels, instructions, validation, and result messages with assistive technology.</li>
      <li>Never rely on color alone to explain an error or a required field.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      See <a href={url("docs/components/feedback/")}>feedback</a> for status messages and the <a
        href={url("docs/accessibility/")}>accessibility checklist</a
      > for consumer responsibilities.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
